# Everything in this repository is executed inside Linux containers, so every
# text file must be checked out with LF endings -- including on Windows.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. A shell script then starts with `#!/bin/bash\r`, and the kernel
# goes looking for an interpreter literally named "/bin/bash\r":
#
#     bash: /scripts/update_hive_metastore.sh: /bin/bash^M:
#           bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# The error names a path nobody wrote, which makes it read like a broken mount
# or a wrong volume rather than a line-ending problem. The same breakage hits
# .sql, .yml and .py files that Spark, dbt and Airflow read inside the
# containers.
#
# `text=auto` still lets Git detect binary files and leave them untouched; the
# explicit rules below are belt and braces for the formats this repo ships.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.pdf binary
*.parquet binary
